What was taken
--------------
The per-(client, endpoint, resolution) coordinate calibration approach:
the record shape, the two independent per-axis least-squares solves, the
degenerate-input guard, the client-identity normalisation, and the
status-line vocabulary (ACTIVE / STALE / ABSENT / DISABLED / UNKNOWN).

What was changed
----------------
Rewritten in Python for this project. The transport was replaced: the
original drove a VNC/RFB connection and drew markers on the remote
framebuffer, whereas this project places markers through the Proxmox
`qm monitor` screendump and applies the transform to `qm sendkey`
coordinates. The store lives under this tool's state directory rather
than `~/.config/vnc-mcp`, so the two projects never share or overwrite
each other's calibration state. Behaviour notes added: the RMSE
acceptability threshold, the tolerant sample parser, and clamping
applied to the mapped point.

Why
---
GUI automation fails in a specific, repeatable way: an agent reads a
screenshot that its viewer has downscaled, then clicks a framebuffer
pixel estimated from that image, and the click lands near the target
rather than on it. Measuring the mapping once per client, endpoint and
resolution is the fix, and the arithmetic is independent of whatever
transport delivers the pixels. No part of the original remains linked;
this is a source-level port under the terms of its licence, which
requires the above notice.
